.TH STORAGE.H 3v32 "October 15, 2026" "v32dev 20261014-dev" "Vircon32 C Library"
.SH NAME
storage.h \- v32dev storage: read and write a file of the host in sectors
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include \(dqstorage.h\(dq
.PP
.BI "storage* storage_init( int " channel " );"
.BI "void     storage_free( storage** " st " );"
.PP
.BI "bool     storage_probe( storage** " st " );"
.BI "bool     storage_wait_ready( storage** " st ", int " frames " );"
.BI "bool     storage_ready( storage** " st " );"
.PP
.BI "int      storage_sector_words( storage** " st " );"
.BI "int      storage_sectors( storage** " st " );"
.BI "int      storage_max_sectors( storage** " st " );"
.BI "bool     storage_readonly( storage** " st " );"
.BI "bool     storage_bytes( storage** " st " );"
.PP
.BI "int      storage_read( storage** " st ", int " sector ", int " count ", int* " dest " );"
.BI "int      storage_write( storage** " st ", int " sector ", int " count ", int* " source " );"
.BI "int      storage_flush( storage** " st " );"
.BI "int      storage_read_words( storage** " st ", int " offset ", int* " dest ", int " count " );"
.BI "int      storage_write_words( storage** " st ", int " offset ", int* " source ", int " count " );"
.PP
.BI "int      storage_read_begin( storage** " st ", int " sector ", int " count ", int* " dest " );"
.BI "int      storage_write_begin( storage** " st ", int " sector ", int " count ", int* " source " );"
.BI "int      storage_flush_begin( storage** " st " );"
.BI "bool     storage_busy( storage** " st " );"
.BI "int      storage_wait( storage** " st " );"
.BI "int      storage_result( storage** " st " );"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
A
.B storage
is a block device (class
.BR V32DEV_CLASS_BLOCK ,
kind
.BR V32DEV_KIND_STORAGE ),
served by the host on a channel of the memory card mailbox (see
.BR v32dev (3v32)).
Typically it is a file of the host computer, a disk image, which the program
can read and write in
.IR sectors ,
and which is the way to get data in and out of the emulator. The core
.BR v32dev.h (3v32)
moves the words; this header sends it requests and interprets the replies, and
includes it.
.PP
The device is made of sectors of a fixed size, announced by the host
.RB ( storage_sector_words ),
numbered from 0. Each word of the program is one word of the device, with one
exception: when the host serves the file in
.I byte mode
.RB ( storage_bytes ),
each word of the device holds one byte, 0 to 255 (a write keeps only the lowest
8 bits), so that a host file such as a text file or a FAT image can be used
directly, one character per word. Otherwise each word is stored as 4 bytes,
little endian, on the host. The host can also serve the file read only
.RB ( storage_readonly ),
and a write then fails with
.BR STORAGE_EROFS .
The host tool serves a file with
.BR "v32dev.py serve game.memc \-\-ch0 storage:FILE[,bytes][,ro]" .
.SS Two ways to use it
.TP
.B Blocking
.BR storage_read ,
.B storage_write
and
.B storage_flush
wait, ending frames, until the request is done. They are the easy way, but the
program does nothing else meanwhile. A request takes at least one frame, and
more if the host is slow to answer. They split a request for more sectors than
the host takes at once (see
.BR storage_max_sectors ).
.TP
.B Not blocking
.BR storage_read_begin ,
.B storage_write_begin
and
.B storage_flush_begin
start a request and return. The program calls
.B storage_probe
on every frame and looks at
.B storage_busy
to see when the request is done, and then at
.B storage_result
for how it went. The program can keep drawing meanwhile.
.PP
Only one request is in progress at a time. A request that is not answered
within
.B STORAGE_TIMEOUT
frames ends with
.BR STORAGE_ETIMEOUT .
.SS By word
.B storage_read_words
and
.B storage_write_words
address the device by word, from any position and in any amount, not by
sector. Whole sectors go straight to or from the program's buffer, and the
parts of sectors at the ends are moved through a sector buffer (a write reads
such a sector first, to keep the rest of its words).
.SS Throughput
The reply to a request has to fit in the ring, so a request moves at most about
2000 words and takes at least one frame. At best that is about 115000 words per
second in word mode (15 sectors of 128 words per request), and about 92000
words per second in byte mode (3 sectors of 512 words per request). The program
keeps one request in flight at a time, so a slow host lowers it.
.SH FUNCTIONS
.TP
.BI "storage_init( int " channel )
Create a storage device on a channel (0 to 3). Returns
.B NULL
if the channel is not valid or memory runs out. It is usable once a probe says
so (see
.BR storage_wait_ready ).
.TP
.BI "storage_free( storage** " st )
Close the channel, release the instance and its buffers and set the pointer to
.BR NULL .
.TP
.BI "storage_probe( storage** " st )
Look at the device for this frame. Call it
.B once on every frame
(the blocking functions do it themselves). It learns the geometry of the device
when the channel opens or the host restarts, takes the replies to the request
in progress, and ends the request if it timed out or the host went away.
Returns true when a request ended, or the device became or stopped being ready.
.TP
.BI "storage_wait_ready( storage** " st ", int " frames )
End frames, probing each time, until the device is usable. Gives up after the
given number of frames and returns false.
.TP
.BI "storage_ready( storage** " st )
True if the device was usable in the latest probe.
.TP
.BI "storage_sector_words( storage** " st )
Words in a sector, as the host announces (128 in word mode, 512 in byte mode
unless the host chose another size). 0 until the device has been ready.
.TP
.BI "storage_sectors( storage** " st )
Sectors in the device.
.TP
.BI "storage_max_sectors( storage** " st )
The most sectors in a request. The blocking functions split bigger ones; the
not blocking ones refuse them with
.BR STORAGE_ERANGE .
.TP
.BI "storage_readonly( storage** " st )
True if the host serves the device read only.
.TP
.BI "storage_bytes( storage** " st )
True if the device is in byte mode.
.TP
.BI "storage_read( storage** " st ", int " sector ", int " count ", int* " dest )
Read
.I count
sectors from the given one into
.IR dest ,
which must have room for
.I count
times
.B storage_sector_words
words. Returns
.B STORAGE_OK
or what failed.
.TP
.BI "storage_write( storage** " st ", int " sector ", int " count ", int* " source )
Write
.I count
sectors from
.IR source .
Returns
.B STORAGE_OK
or what failed.
.TP
.BI "storage_flush( storage** " st )
Ask the host to make sure that everything written is on its storage. Returns
.B STORAGE_OK
or what failed.
.TP
.BI "storage_read_words( storage** " st ", int " offset ", int* " dest ", int " count )
Read
.I count
words starting at word number
.I offset
of the device. Returns
.B STORAGE_OK
or what failed
.RB ( STORAGE_ERANGE
if the words are not all inside the device).
.TP
.BI "storage_write_words( storage** " st ", int " offset ", int* " source ", int " count )
Write
.I count
words starting at word number
.IR offset .
Returns
.B STORAGE_OK
or what failed.
.TP
.BI "storage_read_begin( storage** " st ", int " sector ", int " count ", int* " dest )
Start reading
.I count
sectors (at most
.BR storage_max_sectors )
into
.IR dest ,
which must have room for them and stay valid until the request is done. Returns
.B STORAGE_OK
when it has started, or the reason it did not.
.TP
.BI "storage_write_begin( storage** " st ", int " sector ", int " count ", int* " source )
Start writing sectors. The words are copied right away, so
.I source
can be reused as soon as this returns.
.TP
.BI "storage_flush_begin( storage** " st )
Start asking the host to make sure that everything written is on its storage.
.TP
.BI "storage_busy( storage** " st )
True while a request is in progress.
.TP
.BI "storage_wait( storage** " st )
End frames until the request in progress is done, has failed or has timed out.
Returns its result.
.TP
.BI "storage_result( storage** " st )
The result of the latest request, as of the latest probe.
.B STORAGE_ENODEV
if there is no instance.
.PP
Every function that takes a
.I storage**
accepts a pointer to
.B NULL
and then does nothing (returning false, 0, or
.BR STORAGE_ENODEV ).
.SH VARIABLES
.TP
.B struct storage
A storage device. A program should not need to touch its fields; they are
.IR dev ", " ready ", " sector_words ", " sectors ", " flags ", " max_sectors ,
.IR busy ", " op ", " tag ", " status ", " count ", " buffer ", " deadline ,
.IR reply " and " sector .
.SS Results
Returned by the functions that do something, and by
.BR storage_result :
.TP
.B STORAGE_OK
0. Done.
.TP
.B STORAGE_EINVAL
1. The host did not understand the request.
.TP
.B STORAGE_ERANGE
2. The sectors are outside the device, or more than the host takes in a request.
.TP
.B STORAGE_EROFS
3. The device is read only.
.TP
.B STORAGE_EIO
4. The host could not do it (its file failed), or its reply was not what the
request needs.
.TP
.B STORAGE_ETIMEOUT
5. The host did not answer within
.B STORAGE_TIMEOUT
frames.
.TP
.B STORAGE_ENODEV
6. The device is not usable now (no mailbox, no host, or another class).
.TP
.B STORAGE_EBUSY
7. Another request is in progress.
.TP
.B STORAGE_ENOMEM
8. The program is out of memory.
.TP
.B STORAGE_EABORT
9. The request was lost because the host restarted. Ask again.
.SS Parameters
.TP
.B STORAGE_TIMEOUT
300. Frames to wait for the reply to a request (5 seconds).
.SS Protocol
The words of the descriptor that the host announces, the flags in the third
one, and the requests and replies. A program that uses the functions above does
not need them; they are here for those who write a host. The complete
description is in the protocol document of the project.
.TP
.BR STORAGE_DESC_SECTOR_WORDS ", " STORAGE_DESC_SECTORS ", " STORAGE_DESC_FLAGS ", " STORAGE_DESC_MAX_SECTORS
Descriptor words 0 to 3: words in a sector, sectors in the device, the flags,
and the most sectors in a request.
.TP
.BR STORAGE_FLAG_READONLY ", " STORAGE_FLAG_BYTES
The flags (1 and 2): the device is read only; each word holds one byte.
.TP
.BR STORAGE_OP_READ ", " STORAGE_OP_WRITE ", " STORAGE_OP_FLUSH
The requests (1, 2 and 3). Each has a tag that the reply repeats, so that a late
reply to an earlier request is ignored. READ is tag, sector, count; WRITE is
tag, sector, count and the words; FLUSH is tag.
.TP
.BR STORAGE_REPLY_READ ", " STORAGE_REPLY_WRITE ", " STORAGE_REPLY_FLUSH
The replies (129, 130 and 131): the tag, a result, and for a read the words.
.SH EXAMPLES
Read the first sector of the file, and write it back with the first word
changed. The host is waited for up to 10 seconds, and the result of each call
is checked.
.PP
.RS
.nf
#include "video.h"
#include "storage.h"

void main()
{
    storage* disk = storage_init( 0 );
    int [512] sector;
    int result = STORAGE_ENODEV;

    if( storage_wait_ready( &disk, 600 ) )
    {
        result = storage_read( &disk, 0, 1, sector );
        if( result == STORAGE_OK )
        {
            sector[ 0 ] = sector[ 0 ] + 1;
            result = storage_write( &disk, 0, 1, sector );
        }
        if( result == STORAGE_OK )
            result = storage_flush( &disk );
    }

    while( true )
    {
        clear_screen( color_black );
        if( result == STORAGE_OK )
            print_at( 10, 10, "written" );
        else
            print_at( 10, 10, "failed" );
        end_frame();
    }
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
A program that goes on drawing while a read is in progress:
.PP
.RS
.nf
#include "video.h"
#include "storage.h"

void main()
{
    storage* disk = storage_init( 0 );
    int [512] sector;
    int started = false;
    int frames = 0;

    while( true )
    {
        storage_probe( &disk );

        if( storage_ready( &disk ) && !started )
            started = ( storage_read_begin( &disk, 0, 1, sector ) == STORAGE_OK );

        frames = frames + 1;
        clear_screen( color_black );
        print_at( 10, 10, "reading" );
        end_frame();

        if( started && !storage_busy( &disk ) )
            break;
    }
    storage_free( &disk );

    while( true )
        end_frame();
}
.fi
.RE
.SH NOTES
A part of the file that does not fill a sector is not accessible.
.PP
A host that is killed and comes back, possibly with another file, is noticed:
the geometry is read again, and a request in progress ends with
.BR STORAGE_EABORT .
.PP
The probe must run on every frame while a not blocking request is in progress.
The library allocates with
.BR malloc :
a storage device takes a buffer for one reply and one for a sector, which are
made again if the geometry changes.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR v32dev.h (3v32),
.BR serial.h (3v32),
.BR v32dev (3v32),
.BR vircon32 (3v32)
.SH AUTHOR
Matthew Haas, for the v32io project. The Vircon32 standard library it builds
on was written by Javier Carracedo
.RI ( Carra ).
